---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Slip & Fall Liability by Property Type | slipfallcalculator.com';
const description = 'How premises liability rules differ for retail stores, apartments, government property, and more.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Slip &amp; Fall Liability by Property Type</h1>
  <h2>Retail and Commercial Premises</h2>
  <p>Businesses owe the highest duty of care to customers (invitees). They must regularly inspect, warn of, and remedy hazards. "Reasonable inspection" standards are defined by industry practice and court precedent.</p>
  <h2>Residential Landlord Property</h2>
  <p>Landlords must maintain common areas and make repairs with reasonable promptness after notice. Tenant-caused hazards generally don't create landlord liability.</p>
  <h2>Government Property</h2>
  <p>Sovereign immunity limits suits against government entities. Most states permit claims under tort claims acts, but require short notice deadlines (often 60–180 days) and have damages caps.</p>
  <h2>Social Host (Private Homes)</h2>
  <p>Homeowners owe a duty to guests (licensees) to warn of known hidden hazards. The standard is lower than for commercial invitees.</p>
  <h2>Construction Sites</h2>
  <p>Multiple parties may share liability: general contractor, subcontractor, property owner. OSHA violations can establish negligence per se.</p>
</BaseLayout>
